---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { projects } from '../../data/projects';
export function getStaticPaths() { return projects.map(project => ({ params: { slug: project.slug }, props: { project } })); }
const { project } = Astro.props;
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---
<Layout title={`${project.title} — Adam Morris`} description={project.summary}>
  <article class="container detail">
    <a class="text-link" href={`${base}#projects`}>← All projects</a>
    <h1>{project.title}</h1><p class="lead">{project.summary}</p><p class="eyebrow">{project.category}</p>
    <div class="tags">{project.tags.map(tag => <span>{tag}</span>)}</div>
    {'swarm' in project && project.swarm ? <>
      <p class="project-context">{project.date} · {project.venue}</p>
      <p class="lead">I built a working three-robot patrolling system on Leo Rovers. Each rover maps an arena with lidar SLAM, localises on a shared map and patrols a waypoint graph using a decentralised multi-agent strategy. Robots plan their own routes and share past visits and planned paths over a peer-to-peer Zenoh mesh, without a central planner. The system was tested on real robots under limited communication and silent robot failure.</p>
      <div class="swarm-highlights"><article><strong>3</strong><span>Leo Rovers in the real swarm</span></article><article><strong>3,000</strong><span>random cases cross-checking the ROS 2 planner against the Julia simulator</span></article><article><strong>0.73 s</strong><span>from fleet E-STOP button press to standstill</span></article><article><strong>~20 ms</strong><span>pose request time over the Zenoh mesh</span></article></div>
      <figure class="swarm-media"><video controls muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" poster={`${base}${project.poster}`}><source src={`${base}${project.video}`} type="video/mp4" />Your browser does not support HTML video.</video><figcaption>Baseline patrol (T1), 2 min. Left: overhead camera. Right: the robots’ recorded poses on the SLAM map. Views synchronised by matching logged positions to the camera image.</figcaption></figure>
      <figure class="swarm-media"><img src={`${base}${project.camera}`} alt="Overhead camera view of three Leo Rovers patrolling a fenced arena with box obstacles" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Three Leo Rovers patrolling the experimental arena.</figcaption></figure>
      <section class="detail-section"><h2>System architecture</h2><p>Each rover keeps ROS 2 traffic local. A Zenoh hub on each LattePanda connects directly to the other rover hubs, carrying allow-listed patrol, pose, E-STOP and visualisation traffic. The laptop is optional; the swarm coordinates without it.</p><svg class="architecture-diagram" viewBox="0 0 900 390" role="img" aria-labelledby="fleet-title fleet-desc" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><title id="fleet-title">Three rover and Zenoh mesh architecture</title><desc id="fleet-desc">Three Leo Rovers each have a Raspberry Pi connected to a LattePanda and Zenoh hub. Their hubs form a peer-to-peer mesh. An optional laptop can connect to all three hubs.</desc><g fill="none" stroke="#16808a" stroke-width="3"><path d="M150 160V195M450 160V250M750 160V195M180 210L420 265M720 210L480 265M180 210H720"/></g><g fill="none" stroke="#16808a" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="7 6"><path d="M365 310L168 232M450 310V292M535 310L732 232"/></g><g font-family="Arial,sans-serif" text-anchor="middle" fill="#233840"><g><rect x="20" y="20" width="260" height="140" rx="12" fill="#eaf2f4" stroke="#9bbdc1"/><text x="150" y="48" font-size="18" font-weight="bold">Leo Rover 1</text><text x="150" y="82" font-size="15">Raspberry Pi ↔ LattePanda</text><text x="150" y="112" font-size="15">ROS 2 · Nav2 · RH-ARM</text><text x="150" y="142" font-size="14">rover-local ROS 2</text></g><g><rect x="320" y="20" width="260" height="140" rx="12" fill="#eaf2f4" stroke="#9bbdc1"/><text x="450" y="48" font-size="18" font-weight="bold">Leo Rover 2</text><text x="450" y="82" font-size="15">Raspberry Pi ↔ LattePanda</text><text x="450" y="112" font-size="15">ROS 2 · Nav2 · RH-ARM</text><text x="450" y="142" font-size="14">rover-local ROS 2</text></g><g><rect x="620" y="20" width="260" height="140" rx="12" fill="#eaf2f4" stroke="#9bbdc1"/><text x="750" y="48" font-size="18" font-weight="bold">Leo Rover 3</text><text x="750" y="82" font-size="15">Raspberry Pi ↔ LattePanda</text><text x="750" y="112" font-size="15">ROS 2 · Nav2 · RH-ARM</text><text x="750" y="142" font-size="14">rover-local ROS 2</text></g><circle cx="150" cy="210" r="22" fill="#d9f0ee" stroke="#16808a" stroke-width="2"/><circle cx="450" cy="270" r="22" fill="#d9f0ee" stroke="#16808a" stroke-width="2"/><circle cx="750" cy="210" r="22" fill="#d9f0ee" stroke="#16808a" stroke-width="2"/><text x="150" y="214" font-size="10" font-weight="bold">HUB 1</text><text x="450" y="274" font-size="10" font-weight="bold">HUB 2</text><text x="750" y="214" font-size="10" font-weight="bold">HUB 3</text><rect x="330" y="310" width="240" height="66" rx="10" fill="#eaf2f4" stroke="#9bbdc1"/><text x="450" y="338" font-size="16" font-weight="bold">Optional laptop</text><text x="450" y="360" font-size="13">RViz · runner · recorder · E-STOP</text></g></svg><p class="figure-caption">Three peer-connected rover hubs; no central planner is required.</p></section>
      <section class="detail-section"><h2>Patrolling and collision avoidance</h2><p>RH-ARM aims to minimise idleness: the time since each patrol graph node was last visited. At each node, each robot plans over a receding horizon using A* and discounted idleness reward, treating other robots’ announced paths as future visits. The horizon and discount are set automatically from graph edge times. The ROS 2 / Nav2 port was cross-checked against the original Julia simulator on 3,000 random cases. Credit for the RH-ARM method and original magesim simulator: <a href="https://github.com/jward0/magesim">Dr. James Ward and the magesim project ↗</a></p><ul class="experience-points"><li>Lidar-based Nav2 global and local costmaps avoid static obstacles.</li><li>Robots share pose and velocity at 5 Hz. Each robot creates a 0.35 m keep-out disk around others, including predicted positions at 0.5 s and 1 s.</li><li>A collision monitor slows to 40% when another robot is within 1 m and caps speed so its footprint cannot reach another robot within 2 s.</li><li>Priority-based give-way and escape behaviours address deadlocks. A custom Nav2 behaviour tree replans, clears costmaps, waits, backs up and spins; recovery moves are collision-checked too.</li><li>Two rovers passed head-on in open floor with about 17 cm between their bodies. A corridor too narrow for two robots could deadlock, so the patrol graph avoids narrow two-way corridors.</li></ul></section>
      <section class="detail-section"><h2>Communications, safety and experiments</h2><p>ROS 2 discovery traffic was kept local to each rover, with Zenoh connecting hubs in a full peer-to-peer mesh. Only allow-listed topics and services cross between robots. Idle network traffic dropped from about 2 KB/s to under 100 B/s per rover. A fleet E-STOP travels over the mesh with SSH as fallback; a live GUI monitors fleet health and battery.</p><p>One command runs each test: warm-up, timed recording, failure injection where required, safe stop, return to start and log collection. Tools record node idleness, robot positions, messages and inter-robot separation, then produce summaries and plots. Overhead CCTV was synchronised with robot logs to about 0.5 s by matching logged positions to the camera image.</p><figure class="swarm-media"><img src={`${base}${project.monitor}`} alt="Fleet monitor dashboard showing E-STOP and shutdown controls, rover battery voltages and status for leo1, leo2 and leo3" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Fleet health monitor showing the E-STOP, fleet controls, per-rover battery voltage and status.</figcaption></figure></section>
      <section class="detail-section"><h2>Experiment results</h2><p>Run at Bristol Robotics Laboratory on 30 October 2026, with three robots and a nine-node patrol graph. Values use the first 600 s of each recorded window.</p><div class="results-table-wrap"><table class="results-table"><thead><tr><th>Test</th><th>Condition</th><th>Mean idleness</th><th>Worst idleness</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>T1</th><td>Baseline: every robot hears every message</td><td>25.7 s</td><td>93 s</td></tr><tr><th>T2</th><td>Limited comms: R = 4 m (~53% delivered)</td><td>31.9 s</td><td>136 s</td></tr><tr><th>T3</th><td>Silent failure: one robot stops patrolling and reporting, and parks out of the way</td><td>38.4 s</td><td>123 s</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class="swarm-media"><img src={`${base}${project.results}`} alt="Charts comparing node idleness across baseline, limited-communication and robot-failure tests, and graph mean idleness over time" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Node idleness for T1, T2 and T3 and graph mean idleness over time.</figcaption></figure><ul class="experience-points"><li>Limited communication raised mean idleness by about 24%. Robot estimates of node idleness differed by about 25 s on average in T2, compared with almost 0 in T1.</li><li>With one of three robots silently failed, the two survivors continued patrolling the whole graph without reconfiguration; mean idleness rose by about 50%.</li></ul><p class="honest-caveat">These are single runs. T3 had a shorter (2 min) warm-up, and low batteries limited run length, so results are indicative; repeat runs were planned.</p></section>
      <figure class="swarm-media"><img src={`${base}${project.tracks}`} alt="Paths of three rovers over a 20-minute run, overlaid on a lidar map with a nine-node patrol graph" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Three rover paths over the 20-minute baseline run, overlaid on the SLAM map and nine-node patrol graph.</figcaption></figure>
      <section class="detail-section"><h2>Field lessons</h2><p>Battery sag near 10.4 V cut out onboard computers under load, so scheduling had to account for battery state. Two rovers could stall nose to nose in narrow passages. Mixing DDS implementations across the Zenoh bridge broke service calls, so middleware was standardised per role.</p></section>
      <!-- TODO [CONFIRM]: project start date, role and supervisor/collaborator credit. TODO [CONFIRM]: public repository and paper/report links. -->
    </> : 'real' in project && project.real ? <>
      <figure class="robot-platform"><img src={`${base}projects/pololu-3pi-plus.jpg`} alt="Pololu 3pi+ 32U4 OLED robot with two driven wheels and onboard electronics" width="600" height="480" /><figcaption>Pololu 3pi+ 32U4 OLED robot. Manufacturer reference photo: <a href="https://www.pololu.com/product/4975/pictures">Pololu</a>.</figcaption></figure>
      <p class="project-context">Project {project.number} · Solo and group challenges · Pololu 3pi+ · Arduino (C++)</p>
      <section class="project-part" aria-labelledby="solo-title">
        <p class="eyebrow">PART 1 / SOLO CHALLENGE</p>
        <h2 id="solo-title">Line sensing, kinematics & PID control</h2>
        <p>I implemented the sensing and control components for the individual robotics challenge in Arduino (C++).</p>
        <ul class="experience-points">
          <li>Implemented line sensing using the robot’s onboard infrared sensors.</li>
          <li>Developed kinematics estimation using wheel encoder counts.</li>
          <li>Designed a PID controller for motor and direction control.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="project-video"><div id="video-fallback" class="video-fallback"><p id="video-status" role="status">Loading video preview…</p><a class="button primary" href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NYZzk-loSlc">Watch on YouTube ↗</a></div><iframe id="solo-challenge-video" src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/NYZzk-loSlc?enablejsapi=1" title="Solo robotics challenge video" loading="eager" allow="accelerometer; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share" allowfullscreen referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin"></iframe></div>
        <a class="text-link" href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NYZzk-loSlc">Watch the solo challenge on YouTube ↗</a>
      </section>
      <section class="project-part" aria-labelledby="group-title">
        <p class="eyebrow">PART 2 / GROUP CHALLENGE</p>
        <h2 id="group-title">Evolutionary PID tuning</h2>
        <p>In the group task, we investigated automatic wheel-speed controller tuning using a genetic algorithm, testing constant and reversing speed demands on hardware.</p>
      <figure class="project-evidence"><img src={`${base}${project.image}`} alt="Measured wheel speed and control error during a direction reversal, from Figure 5 of the project report" width="800" height="390" /><figcaption>Controller response during direction reversal. Figure 5 from the project report; example fitness score: 0.04.</figcaption></figure>
      <section class="detail-section"><h2>The challenge</h2><p>{project.challenge}</p></section>
      <section class="detail-section"><h2>My contribution</h2><p>I led the code implementation in Arduino (C++) within a three-person team. The wider project combined controller tuning, evolutionary optimisation, hardware experiments and analysis of wheel-speed responses.</p></section>
      <section class="detail-section"><h2>The approach</h2><p>{project.approach}</p></section>
      <section class="detail-section"><h2>Results</h2><p>{project.outcome}</p></section>
      <section class="detail-section"><h2>Scope & limitations</h2><p>{project.limitations}</p></section>
      <section class="detail-section"><h2>Project report</h2><p>Application of a genetic algorithm to optimise parameter values of a PID controller</p><a class="text-link" href={`${base}${project.report}`}>Read the group report (PDF) ↗</a></section>
      </section>
    </> : <>
      <div class={`project-image ${project.type}`}><span class="image-index">PROJECT / {project.number}</span><span class="image-placeholder">[ PROJECT PHOTO / DIAGRAM ]</span><span class="image-caption">{project.type.toUpperCase()} STUDY</span></div>
      <p class="placeholder-note">Illustrative project. Details to be added.</p>
      <section class="detail-section"><h2>The challenge</h2><p>[ {project.challenge} ]</p></section>
      <section class="detail-section"><h2>The approach</h2><p>[ {project.approach} ]</p></section>
      <section class="detail-section"><h2>Results & reflections</h2><p>[ {project.outcome} ]</p></section>
    </>}
    <div class="actions"><a class="button primary" href={`${base}#contact`}>Discuss this project ↗</a><a class="text-link" href={`${base}#projects`}>Back to projects</a></div>
  </article>
  <script is:inline>
    (() => {
      const frame = document.getElementById('solo-challenge-video');
      if (!frame) return;
      const fallback = document.getElementById('video-fallback');
      const status = document.getElementById('video-status');
      const unavailable = () => {
        fallback.hidden = false;
        frame.style.visibility = 'hidden';
        status.textContent = 'Video preview unavailable here. Watch the demonstration on YouTube.';
      };
      const timeout = setTimeout(unavailable, 8000);
      let speedRequested = false;
      const setDefaultSpeed = (event) => {
        if (!speedRequested && event.target.getAvailablePlaybackRates().includes(2)) {
          speedRequested = true;
          event.target.setPlaybackRate(2);
        }
      };
      const initialise = () => new YT.Player(frame.id, {
        events: {
          onReady: (event) => {
            clearTimeout(timeout);
            fallback.hidden = true;
            frame.style.visibility = 'visible';
            setDefaultSpeed(event);
          },
          onError: () => { clearTimeout(timeout); unavailable(); },
          onStateChange: (event) => {
            if (event.data === YT.PlayerState.PLAYING) setDefaultSpeed(event);
          }
        }
      });
      if (window.YT && window.YT.Player) initialise();
      else {
        const previous = window.onYouTubeIframeAPIReady;
        window.onYouTubeIframeAPIReady = () => { if (previous) previous(); initialise(); };
        if (!document.querySelector('script[src="https://www.youtube.com/iframe_api"]')) {
          const api = document.createElement('script');
          api.src = 'https://www.youtube.com/iframe_api';
          document.head.appendChild(api);
        }
      }
    })();
  </script>
</Layout>
